(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles for three weeks. He started amlodipine for hypertension one month ago. There is no warmth, redness, tenderness or skin discoloration, and he has no history of heart, kidney or liver disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bilateral deep vein thrombosis","Drug-induced edema","Chronic venous insufficiency","Lymphedema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles that began after starting amlodipine, with no warmth, redness or tenderness, fits drug-induced edema. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine commonly cause it; the swelling is often bilateral and usually shows no signs of inflammation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with Mobitz type II block found on a routine tracing declines a pacemaker because he feels perfectly well. Which risk should be explained to him?","choices":["Gradual return to normal","Progression to complete block","Easing into first-degree block","Atrial fibrillation and stroke"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II usually originates at the bundle of His, leaving a weak and unreliable escape rhythm, and carries a higher risk of progression to complete heart block and sudden cardiac death. It is considered dangerous even without symptoms because of its unpredictable progression, so a permanent pacemaker is recommended. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections was treated for pyelonephritis a month ago. She now has low-grade fevers, fatigue and a new murmur, and blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Enterococci","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Viridans streptococci","Streptococcus gallolyticus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her recurrent urinary tract infections and recent pyelonephritis point to the genitourinary tract, the source of enterococci. Enterococci are a subacute and native valve cause, which fits her low-grade fevers, fatigue and new murmur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had a resting heart rate of 105 to 115 for a year. Thyroid tests, a blood count, a drug screen and an echocardiogram are normal, and she takes no medications or stimulants. Her electrocardiogram shows a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Focal atrial tachycardia","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A resting rate of 105 to 115 for a year with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus tachycardia, and normal thyroid tests, blood count, drug screen and echocardiogram with no medications or stimulants exclude the secondary causes. A sinus tachycardia that stays unexplained after that is inappropriate sinus tachycardia, a diagnosis of exclusion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman without diabetes has ulcers on the posterior aspect of both lower legs. Her pedal pulses are strong, and there are no varicose veins, brown pigmentation or leg swelling. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Arterial ulcer","Diabetic neuropathic ulcer","Venous ulcer","Erythema induratum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral ulcers on the posterior aspect of the lower legs are the pattern of erythema induratum on the differential of leg ulcers. Her strong pulses argue against arterial ulcers, the lack of diabetes against neuropathic ulcers, and the absence of varicose veins, pigmentation and swelling against venous disease. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a 4.8 cm descending thoracic aortic aneurysm, and he now has back pain that is attributed to the aneurysm. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Repair only above 6.0 cm","Repair the aneurysm","Imaging every 3 to 5 years","Imaging every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repair is recommended for all symptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysms. His 4.8 cm descending aneurysm is below the asymptomatic threshold of 6.0 cm, but back pain attributed to the aneurysm makes it symptomatic, and that alone indicates repair. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy and marked biatrial enlargement has had palpitations for a week, and the electrocardiogram now shows atrial fibrillation. Which treatment reduces her risk of embolism?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","A positive inotrope","Aggressive diuresis","Anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In this woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy, marked biatrial enlargement and new atrial fibrillation, anticoagulants may be needed to prevent embolism from atrial thrombi. Beta blockers or verapamil and diltiazem may help filling time, but only anticoagulation addresses the embolic risk. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has sudden palpitations after three energy drinks. She notices her neck throbbing, and examination shows a frog sign (rapid visible pulsations in the neck). Her heart rate is 172 and regular, with a narrow QRS and no visible P waves. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sudden, regular narrow tachycardia at 172 with hidden P waves, neck pulsations (frog sign) and a caffeine trigger in a young woman without heart disease is atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia. It is a reentry circuit within the node, far more common in women, and usually runs at 140 to 180. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman being treated for an acute aortic dissection develops a new diastolic murmur and worsening breathlessness. Which complication explains these findings?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Carotid stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection can cause aortic regurgitation through dilatation of the aortic root, alongside its other presenting features of tearing chest pain, syncope and hypertension or hypotension. A new diastolic murmur with worsening breathlessness during treatment for a dissection fits that complication. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis brings an old wallet card that lists clindamycin for her dental prophylaxis. She has a penicillin allergy. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["She no longer needs any","Keep using clindamycin","Clindamycin is dropped","Switch to amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection. Her previous endocarditis still qualifies her, so with a penicillin allergy she receives azithromycin or clarithromycin, doxycycline, or cephalexin if her reaction was not anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has had a peripheral intravenous catheter in his right forearm for six days. He now has a fever of 39.2 degrees Celsius, a tender cord along the vein, purulent drainage at the insertion site, and redness that extends several centimeters beyond the vein margin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Cellulitis alone","Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A fever of 39.2 degrees Celsius, purulent drainage and redness extending several centimeters beyond the vein margin after six days of cannulation indicate infection within the vein, suppurative or septic thrombophlebitis. Septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without venous cannulation, which he has. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with known obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border that radiates to the suprasternal notch. At the bedside, the examiner tests how the murmur responds to changes in blood flow through the heart. Which maneuver would make his murmur louder?","choices":["Valsalva maneuver","Passive leg raise","Sustained handgrip","Rapid squatting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anything that decreases blood flow in the heart increases outflow obstruction and makes the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy louder. The Valsalva maneuver and standing decrease preload, and amyl nitrite decreases afterload, so each increases this crescendo-decrescendo murmur. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Acute pericarditis accounts for about what share of emergency department admissions for nonischemic chest pain?","choices":["About 1%","About 5%","About 25%","About 50%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis is recorded in about 5% of patients admitted to the emergency department for nonischemic chest pain, with an annual incidence of about 27.7 per 100,000 people. Of those patients, about 15 to 30% go on to recurrent pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which lifestyle measures are advised for adults with cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Caffeine avoidance and deliberate weight gain","Sodium restriction and alcohol cessation","Strict bed rest and total fluid restriction","Fluid loading and a high-salt diet plan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The adult lifestyle modifications for cardiomyopathy are sodium restriction and alcohol cessation. They sit alongside guideline-directed medical therapy (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, beta blockers, angiotensin receptor blockers, diuretics), device therapy and, in advanced cases, an assist device or transplant. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which anticoagulation strategy is described as the classical, waning approach?","choices":["Heparin bridged to warfarin","Apixaban with a loading period","Parenteral therapy, then dabigatran","Oral rivaroxaban with loading"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classical, but waning, strategy starts with heparin and then bridges to warfarin. The two newer strategies are parenteral therapy switched after 5 days to dabigatran or edoxaban, and oral monotherapy with rivaroxaban or apixaban using a loading dose then a maintenance dose. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl nearly fainted twice while standing in the lunch line; both times she felt better once she lay down. She plays competitive soccer. A systolic murmur is heard at the left sternal border that becomes louder with the Valsalva maneuver. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Clear her to play next week","Recheck the murmur in a year","Urgent echocardiography","Reassure; common in teenagers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presyncope that occurs on standing and is relieved by lying down identifies patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy at high risk of sudden death, so the work-up is urgent. Her murmur that grows louder with the Valsalva maneuver and her competitive soccer add to the concern, and echocardiography begins that urgent work-up. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man received chest radiation for lymphoma 20 years ago. He has 8 months of exertional dyspnea, fatigue, leg edema and ascites. His jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (it rises on inspiration instead of falling), and a pericardial knock (an early diastolic sound, slightly earlier than a third heart sound) is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Infective endocarditis","Acute pericarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eight months of fluid overload (edema, ascites) and diminished cardiac output (fatigue, exertional dyspnea), an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock fit constrictive pericarditis. Prior chest radiation is a listed cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with claudication, diabetes and continued smoking is taking a statin. Which low-density lipoprotein target applies to him?","choices":["Below 70 mg/dL","Below 100 mg/dL","Below 130 mg/dL","Below 160 mg/dL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lipid-lowering therapy in peripheral artery disease targets a low-density lipoprotein below 100 mg/dL, and below 70 mg/dL for high-risk patients such as those with diabetes or continued smoking. He has both, so his target is below 70 mg/dL. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Uremic pericarditis is typically associated with a blood urea nitrogen above which level?","choices":["10 mg/dL","20 mg/dL","60 mg/dL","200 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uremia is a listed cause of pericarditis, tied to a blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL. It sits among the noninfectious causes alongside post-infarction disease, autoimmune disease such as lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, neoplasm and iatrogenic injury. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has recovered from mild viral myocarditis and feels well at discharge. He asks whether he needs any long-term follow-up with a cardiologist. Which later complication does follow-up watch for?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with mild myocarditis recover completely, as he has, but one third subsequently develop dilated cardiomyopathy. That later risk is what long-term follow-up with a cardiologist watches for. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 appears in someone with a known accessory pathway. Which intravenous drug is used?","choices":["Procainamide","Magnesium","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 in someone with a known accessory pathway is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. When Wolff-Parkinson-White is known, intravenous procainamide is used, and adenosine must never be given. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 2 weeks with a peripherally inserted central line. She develops fever, and two separate blood cultures grow a gram-positive coccus with no other source. Which organism causes the largest share of this setting's native valve endocarditis?","choices":["Enterococci","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has been hospitalized for 2 weeks with a central line, making this health care-associated native valve endocarditis, and Staphylococcus aureus causes about 52% of those cases (versus about 28% of community-acquired cases). Methicillin resistance is common among these strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for acute myocarditis?","choices":["Long antibiotics","Early transplant","Septal ablation","Supportive care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care is the mainstay of treatment for acute myocarditis, directed toward the clinical scenario. Some patients need aggressive hemodynamic stabilization, and medications are added as needed: vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, diuretics, antiarrhythmics and anticoagulation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on chemotherapy for breast cancer comes to the emergency department with a calf 4 cm larger than the other, tenderness along the deep veins and no better explanation, so her Wells score is high. Her D-dimer, drawn at triage, is negative. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Ordering a venous duplex ultrasound","Measuring both calf circumferences","Asking about shortness of breath","Ending the work-up at the D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, calf swelling over 3 cm and deep venous tenderness give her a high Wells score. In high-risk patients the D-dimer is optional, and even a negative result leads on to ultrasound of the thigh, so stopping at the D-dimer would leave a likely deep vein thrombosis (DVT) unexamined. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who started a new antibiotic three days ago faints briefly. Her electrocardiogram before the event showed a markedly prolonged QT. The monitor now shows a wide QRS tachycardia whose peaks rise and fall so that the complexes seem to twist around the baseline. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Coarse ventricular fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Torsades de pointes is a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia in which the QRS complexes appear to twist around the baseline, and a long QT is the risk factor. Her new antibiotic and markedly prolonged QT fit, and her brief faint shows how the rhythm can cause syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is brought in confused, with a blood pressure of 76/40. The monitor shows a slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus bradycardia, and confusion with a blood pressure of 76/40 make it acute, symptomatic and hemodynamically unstable. Atropine is first line; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old cyclist with no symptoms has a rhythm in which the PR interval lengthens beat by beat until a QRS complex is dropped, after which the cycle starts again. Echocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["No treatment needed","Catheter ablation","Transcutaneous pacing","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A PR that lengthens beat by beat until a QRS drops, then restarts, is Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block, which is usually asymptomatic. With no symptoms and a normal echocardiogram, no treatment is needed; a permanent pacemaker is considered only for symptomatic patients, and increased vagal tone is a named cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Low potassium, low magnesium","High potassium, low sodium","Low calcium, high sodium","High phosphate, low chloride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, along with hypercalcemia, are the electrolyte imbalances named as causes of premature ventricular contractions, together with hypoxemia, alcohol, caffeine and stimulants. These beats occur with or without structural heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 4-year-old with cyanotic congenital heart disease and a systemic-to-pulmonary shunt has 2 weeks of fever. The murmur sounds unchanged. Which finding would most suggest that the shunt is infected?","choices":["An unchanged murmur","Falling oxygen saturation","Rising blood pressure","Osler nodes on fingers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In children with cyanotic congenital heart disease and systemic-pulmonary shunts, the murmur may not change; instead, falling oxygen saturation can signal graft infection. This 4-year-old's 2 weeks of fever with an unchanged murmur makes saturation the sign to watch. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has new pauses and slow rates on an ambulatory monitor. He started verapamil and digoxin last month for palpitations. He feels mildly lightheaded but is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Stop the rate-slowing drugs","Intravenous atropine now","Permanent pacemaker now","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. New pauses and slow rates a month after starting verapamil, a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, and digoxin fit drug-induced sick sinus syndrome, since those medications are listed causes. Management starts with treating the underlying cause, and he is stable and only mildly lightheaded, so stopping the rate-slowing drugs comes first. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 13, 22"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man who smokes wakes most nights with burning pain in his forefoot and toes that eases when he hangs the leg over the bed; he now sleeps in a recliner. Which ankle-brachial index range would be expected?","choices":["0.4 to 0.9","0 to 0.4","Above 0.9 to 1.3","Above 1.3"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nightly forefoot and toe pain eased by dependency, so that he now sleeps in a recliner, is ischemic rest pain of critical limb ischemia. Rest pain and tissue loss, ulcer or gangrene, correspond to an ankle-brachial index of 0 to 0.4, below the claudication band of 0.4 to 0.9. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 4.7 cm on his latest ultrasound. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["Emergency surgery this evening","None until it reaches 5.5 cm","Vascular referral, for observation","Cardiology, for echocardiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An aortic aneurysm of 4.5 cm or larger warrants vascular specialist referral for observation and assessment, so his asymptomatic 4.7 cm aneurysm qualifies. Urgent referral applies whenever there is aneurysmal pain, and surgical referral is considered at 5.0 cm or larger. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman was admitted with chest pain after a near drowning. Her electrocardiogram showed ST elevation with T-wave inversion, and echocardiography showed hypokinesis of the midsegment and apex of the left ventricle. Which catheterization finding is expected if this is stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Normal coronary arteries","Triple-vessel narrowing","A proximal coronary occlusion","A ruptured coronary plaque"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A 70-year-old woman with chest pain after a near drowning, ST elevation with T wave inversion, and hypokinesis of the midsegment and apex of the left ventricle fits stress cardiomyopathy. The diagnosis is confirmed with cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in cardiac arrest is intubated by the team. Compressions continue. How should breaths now be given?","choices":["One breath every 2 seconds","Compressions without breaths","Two breaths per 30 compressions","One breath every 6 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once the patient is intubated, cardiopulmonary resuscitation continues as continuous chest compressions with 1 breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths per minute). Compressions no longer pause for breaths, which keeps interruptions to a minimum. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for stable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion","A vagal maneuver","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver. Adenosine follows, and may be repeated once, to convert the rhythm or slow it enough to see P waves; a hemodynamically unstable patient instead gets immediate synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia treated, even when the patient has a pulse?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Observation on a monitor","Intravenous lidocaine alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Even when the patient has a pulse and looks well, polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable; it is a short-lived transitional state. It is defibrillated, followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization, and intravenous metoprolol or amiodarone may be added. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In high-income countries, which conditions are now the leading predisposing conditions for infective endocarditis?","choices":["Hypertension and high cholesterol","Rheumatic heart disease and syphilis","Atrial fibrillation and heart block","Degenerative valve disease and devices"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Degenerative valve disease and intracardiac devices are now the leading predisposing conditions in high-income countries. Other listed risk factors include congenital and rheumatic heart disease, prosthetic valves and injection drug use, and people over 60 account for more than 50% of cases. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a cold 10 days ago. He now has sharp, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) substernal chest pain that is worse lying flat and eases when he leans forward. A pericardial friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) is heard at the left sternal border, and the electrocardiogram shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression and ST depression in aVR. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pericarditis","Anterior myocardial infarction","Viral pleuritis","Acute pulmonary embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sharp pleuritic substernal pain that is worse supine and eased by leaning forward, a friction rub at the left sternal border, and widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression and reciprocal ST depression in aVR meet three of the four criteria; two suffice. A preceding cold fits, since most idiopathic cases are likely viral. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 14, 17, 19"},{"q":"What is the common site of a pseudoaneurysm?","choices":["Popliteal artery after bypass","Femoral artery after catheterization","Carotid bulb after endarterectomy","Infrarenal aorta in smokers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The common site of a pseudoaneurysm is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, caused by laceration of the artery by the access needle. The leak forms a blood-filled cavity outside the vessel wall that seals as it thromboses. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a stab wound to the abdomen arrives pulseless after losing a large amount of blood. The monitor shows an organized narrow rhythm. Compressions are underway. Besides compressions and epinephrine, which problem must be addressed urgently?","choices":["Hypothermia","Toxin ingestion","Hyperkalemia","Hypovolemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pulseless patient with an organized narrow rhythm has pulseless electrical activity, which is unshockable. Its two leading causes in adults are hypovolemia and hypoxia, and her large blood loss from a stab wound is hypovolemia that must be corrected along with compressions and epinephrine. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has abrupt-onset palpitations during a week of little sleep and heavy coffee. Her heart rate is 160 and regular, and every QRS is preceded by an identical P wave whose shape differs from her baseline P wave. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Sinus tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia at 160 with one identical P wave before each QRS, shaped differently from her baseline P wave, comes from a single ectopic focus in the atria outside the sinus node: focal atrial tachycardia. It is commonly seen without heart disease when metabolic demand rises, and most people report abrupt-onset palpitations, as she does. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman had a bioprosthetic aortic valve placed 5 months ago. She now has a fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur, and blood cultures have been drawn. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Penicillin G plus gentamicin","Vancomycin, gentamicin and cefepime","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Ceftriaxone alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her bioprosthetic valve was placed 5 months ago, so it has been in place 1 year or less, and empiric therapy for prosthetic valve endocarditis in that window is vancomycin plus gentamicin plus cefepime. Empiric therapy covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci and starts only after cultures are drawn. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man on the cardiology ward develops jugular venous distension and a blood pressure of 88/60 a day after a catheter procedure. Before drainage, which bedside triad should be specifically checked for?","choices":["Fever, a new murmur and splinters","Hypotension, neck veins, muffled sounds","Hypertension, bradycardia, apnea","Alternans, Kussmaul sign, a knock"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New jugular venous distension and a blood pressure of 88/60 the day after a catheter procedure raise concern for tamponade. The Beck triad is distant (muffled) heart sounds, distended jugular veins and decreased arterial pressure, so he already shows two of the three, and muffled heart sounds should be checked. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a first deep vein thrombosis is otherwise healthy and hemodynamically stable. The plan is oral anticoagulation monotherapy with a loading phase followed by maintenance, without any injection lead-in. Which drug fits this strategy?","choices":["Fondaparinux","Edoxaban","Dabigatran","Apixaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral monotherapy with a loading dose followed by a maintenance dose, with no injection lead-in, is the strategy that uses rivaroxaban or apixaban, both anti-Xa agents. Apixaban (Eliquis) is a direct factor Xa inhibitor, and he is stable and otherwise healthy, so the oral-only plan fits. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"An 82-year-old man is confused, with a blood pressure of 74/40 and a heart rate of 32. The electrocardiogram shows P waves and wide QRS complexes at two separate regular rates with no relationship between them. Which is the most appropriate immediate measure?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing","Intravenous amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. P waves and wide QRS complexes at two unrelated rates with a ventricular rate of 32 are complete heart block, and confusion with a blood pressure of 74/40 make him unstable. Emergent options are atropine, epinephrine and transcutaneous pacing, which bridges him to the definitive permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who takes no anticoagulant develops sudden, severe pain in her left leg two hours ago. The leg is cold and pale, pulses are absent below the femoral artery, and she has numbness over the dorsum of the foot. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia","Acute embolic limb ischemia","Intermittent claudication","Atheroembolism with blue toes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden severe pain with a cold, pale leg and absent pulses below the femoral artery is acute limb ischemia, sudden occlusion of a previously patent artery. Thromboemboli are most common and the heart is the source in 80 to 90% of cases, with atrial fibrillation a named source; numbness over the dorsum of the foot is an early neurologic sign. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is diagnosed with a first proximal deep vein thrombosis five days after knee replacement under two hours of general anesthesia. His clot is attributed to the venous stasis and endothelial trauma of the Virchow triad, both from the surgery. What duration of anticoagulation is appropriate?","choices":["Indefinitely","Six weeks","Three to six months","Three months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Knee replacement under two hours of general anesthesia is surgery under general anesthesia for more than 30 minutes, a major transient, reversible risk factor. A first venous thromboembolism (VTE) provoked by a major transient factor is treated for 3 months, which is also the minimum for any first VTE. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had three episodes of pericarditis in 18 months, each treated in primary care with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Gastroenterology","Infectious disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three episodes in 18 months is recurrent pericarditis, and recurrences are a stated reason to refer to cardiology for further assessment. The other referral triggers are no response to conservative management and development of constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner is diagnosed with acute viral pericarditis and starts ibuprofen and colchicine. He asks when he can return to training. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid exercise for the rest of his life","Resume running once the chest pain stops","Run on alternate days during treatment","Avoid strenuous activity for at least 1 month"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous physical activity such as marathon training is avoided until symptoms resolve and biomarkers such as C-reactive protein normalize, for at least 1 month after diagnosis, with a maximal heart rate under 100 beats per minute. He can return once all of those conditions are met. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has needed prednisone for several months for pericarditis that did not respond to first-line therapy. Which complication of her treatment should be specifically anticipated?","choices":["Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia","Osteoporosis","Hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids increase the risk of osteoporosis, and in a 76-year-old on months of prednisone that risk should be specifically anticipated. Older adults also call for caution with anti-inflammatory drugs on the Beers list, with colchicine considered for dose reduction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy drinks most of a bottle of whiskey every night. He is on a beta blocker and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, his ejection fraction is 30%, and he asks whether anything he does himself could help his heart recover. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["Damage is fixed once the heart dilates","Stopping alcohol may let function recover","Wine would be safer than spirits for him","Only a transplant could restore function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Long-term excess alcohol consumption is a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy, and cardiac function can recover if alcohol intake is reduced or halted. For this man, who drinks most of a bottle of whiskey nightly, stopping alcohol is the step he can take himself, alongside his beta blocker and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a new moderate pericardial effusion with joint aches and fatigue. She has not traveled and has no recent viral illness. Which test is most reasonable to add to her workup?","choices":["Blood cultures","Hemoglobin A1c","Serum lipase","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The effusion workup includes a complete blood count, chemistry with renal function, thyroid-stimulating hormone and troponins, and possibly an antinuclear antibody, for example in a young woman with an effusion. At 27, with joint aches and fatigue and no travel or viral illness, she is that patient. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with advanced dilated cardiomyopathy has refractory symptoms on full medical therapy. Severe lung disease and frailty rule him out as a transplant candidate, and he wants to remain at home and mobile. Which treatment option fits his situation?","choices":["Surgical myectomy of the septum","Urgent listing for cardiac transplant","Assist device as destination therapy","Assist device as a bridge to transplant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A left ventricular assist device (LVAD), a pump that helps the left ventricle in heart failure, is used as destination therapy for patients who are not candidates for cardiac transplant, as his lung disease and frailty make him. Patients may stay alive over 5 years with the device. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes Osler nodes from Janeway lesions?","choices":["Osler nodes are painless","Osler nodes persist for months","Osler nodes are painful","Osler nodes are retinal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes are painful lesions on the palms, soles and distal phalanges that appear in crops and fade in 2-3 days, from septic emboli or immune complexes. Janeway lesions are nontender hemorrhagic lesions on the palms and soles from septic emboli, so pain is the distinguishing feature. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has 4 days of fever to 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), rigors and a cough. A new holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and he looks toxic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral valve endocarditis","Community-acquired pneumonia","Tricuspid valve endocarditis","Acute viral pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a person who injects drugs, fever, rigors and a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border point to tricuspid valve endocarditis, the valve named for this group (Staphylococcus aureus causes 80-90% of their tricuspid infections); the lung, a listed embolic site, explains the cough. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 11, 18"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man is recovering from a carotid endarterectomy for high-grade symptomatic stenosis. Which complications of the procedure should be monitored for?","choices":["Rupture into the retroperitoneum","Stroke or nerve damage","Graft infection of the leg","Wet gangrene of the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carotid endarterectomy removes plaque from inside the artery through a surgical incision and is the gold standard for high-grade stenosis. Its named complications are stroke or nerve damage, so his recovery should be watched for new neurologic deficits. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has a preoperative electrocardiogram before cataract surgery. He feels well. His heart rate is 70, every P wave is followed by a narrow QRS complex, and the PR interval is constant at 0.26 seconds on every beat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["First-degree block","Mobitz type I block","Mobitz type II block","Complete heart block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A constant PR interval longer than 0.20 seconds (here 0.26) with every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS, at a regular rate of 70, is first-degree atrioventricular block. The prolonged PR can occur along with an underlying rhythm. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has atrial fibrillation found at a routine visit. Her heart rate is 84, she has no symptoms of instability, and her blood pressure is 128/76. Which is the most appropriate rate management?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Atropine","Oral rate control","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She is hemodynamically stable (blood pressure 128/76, no instability) and a rate of 84 is not a rapid ventricular response, so she gets rate control; the intravenous route is kept for stable patients in rapid ventricular response. Anticoagulation is then weighed for her, guided by her CHA2DS2-VASc stroke-risk score. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with a first calf deep vein thrombosis is hemodynamically stable, has low bleeding risk and normal kidney function, and has a supportive spouse who will bring him to every follow-up visit. Which is the best setting for treatment?","choices":["Observation without treatment","Hospital admission for heparin","Intensive care unit monitoring","Outpatient anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy is appropriate for patients who are hemodynamically stable, have low bleeding risk and no renal insufficiency, and have good support and reliable follow-up. He meets every criterion, and his clot is in the calf, not an iliofemoral clot that would require admission. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has frequent premature junctional contractions, more than 10 per minute, on a monitor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance alone","Check for heart disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Premature junctional contractions that are frequent, more than 10 per minute or multifocal, prompt a search for underlying structural heart disease, and the beats are then treated with a beta blocker. Her rate of more than 10 per minute meets that threshold. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"}]};
